If a clinician treats a patient in a supervised-setting and the patient receives a regular-buprenorphine-dosage then the clinician may add a short-acting-full-agonist-opioid to the regular-buprenorphine-dosage for the patient and may continue the regular-buprenorphine-dosage for the patient.
If a clinician makes a buprenorphine-discontinuation-decision for a patient then the clinician should closely monitor the patient and should monitor the patient for an oversedation and should monitor the patient for a respiratory-depression.
If a clinician makes a buprenorphine-discontinuation-decision for a patient then the patient can possibly require a high-dose-full-agonist-opioid.
If a clinician makes a buprenorphine-discontinuation-decision for a patient and the patient receives a high-dose-full-agonist-opioid then the high-dose-full-agonist-opioid can potentially cause an oversedation for the patient and can potentially cause a respiratory-depression for the patient.
